#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101702} :activity/id #uuid "40df638a-5935-51ad-9df2-3b9723b349d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085 :topic/id #uuid "2c0b4af5-6010-5e83-8fcc-56c647d1ade5" :topic/math-academy-id 1254} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101971 :tutorial/content "Now that we're familiar with graph translations, reflections, and stretches, it's time to combine these transformations. \n\nWe start by considering a general transformation of the form\n\n$$\ny = af\\left(b(x+c)\\right)+d\n$$\n\nLike the order of operations in arithmetic, the order in which transformations are applied matters. In general, the order *must* be as follows:\n\n1\\. Horizontal stretch\n\n2\\. Horizontal translation\n\n3\\. Vertical stretch\n\n4\\. Vertical translation\n\nIf that seems like a lot to take in, we have the following rule of thumb that can help us to remember the order in which to apply the transformations:\n\n- We start by performing the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ This means we first deal with the $b(x+c)$ part. So, we *first* carry out the horizontal stretch, *followed by* the horizontal translation.\n\n- Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ So, we *first* carry out the vertical stretch, *followed by* the vertical translation.\n\nFinally, note that not every transformation will necessarily be present. In this lesson, we will consider a maximum of two graph transformations." :tutorial/id #uuid "a2fc2824-fd51-5bc6-8c19-7bbf7b92a6c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3287 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f02fd6ea-5c46-55f5-9d51-710cd19982f3" :step/math-academy-id 18036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100710 :question/id #uuid "34af9714-5109-59b7-90cb-